// EmbeddingMode controls which retrieval representation is computed.
//
// BGE-M3 supports all four modes from a single model checkpoint, avoiding
// the need to load separate models for hybrid search pipelines.
//
// Cloud backends only support DENSE. The server MUST return UNIMPLEMENTED
// if SPARSE, MULTI_VECTOR, or HYBRID is requested against a cloud backend.
//
//	DENSE:
//	  Standard single-vector representation. Fast and compact. Use as
//	  default for semantic search and content gap analysis topic encoding.
//	  Output: one DenseVector per input text.
//
//	SPARSE:
//	  Learned token-level importance weights. Similar to BM25 but learned
//	  rather than rule-based. Excels at exact keyword matching and
//	  out-of-domain queries. Particularly effective for German compound
//	  words and rare domain-specific terms that dense vectors may miss.
//	  Output: one SparseVector (indices + values) per input text.
//
//	MULTI_VECTOR:
//	  ColBERT-style late interaction. Produces one vector per token rather
//	  than one per document. At query time each query token is matched
//	  against all document tokens and maximum similarities are summed
//	  (MaxSim). Highest retrieval quality but most expensive to store and
//	  query. Recommended for re-scoring borderline content gap candidates
//	  near the similarity threshold.
//	  Output: one MultiVector (token_vectors[]) per input text.
//
//	HYBRID:
//	  Returns dense + sparse in a single round trip. Use when indexing into
//	  Qdrant with hybrid search enabled. Avoids two gRPC calls per batch,
//	  halving network overhead during corpus ingestion.
//	  Output: one HybridVector (dense + sparse) per input text.
